					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/archonoid" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(476311); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=476311&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609189595" alt="Archonoid" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=476311)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/archonoid" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(476311); return false;" >Archonoid</a></span> <span class="odate time_1363655110 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Mar 2013 01:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1733492">
									

<p>I kind of agree with this. There's a fairly strong trope of (bored + immortal = depravity), which makes the ending a bit predictable. Of course, tropes are not inherently bad. 1733 also uses that trope, and it does it quite well.</p>
<p>Just as something to consider if you do want to change it: what if that's not a necessary outcome? You could do that in a few different ways. One is to simply not have the couple go into the flesh-rending, crime-committing direction. There are other aspects of &quot;immortal shared experience&quot; that could be investigated; you touch, for example, on a shared body language being developed - this could become manifest in a full-fledged synchronicity of actions. Perhaps even a blurring of separate identities into a psuedo-hive-mind.</p>
<p>Another option would be to go through all the body horror stuff - then come out the other side, and go back to picnics in the park. Are there any interesting implications to a couple being able to do all those profoundly bizarre things and then go back to &quot;normal&quot; experiences?</p>

							</div>

								
					
